1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,512
Railvanta.

2
00:00:01,512 --> 00:00:05,188
A sandbox payment orchestrator that
makes the hard payment failures

3
00:00:05,188 --> 00:00:08,650
reproducible, and proves
how each one is handled.

4
00:00:10,175 --> 00:00:11,600
When an app takes a payment,

5
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,688
there is a gap between:
we sent the charge,

6
00:00:13,688 --> 00:00:15,388
and: we know what happened.

7
00:00:15,388 --> 00:00:16,975
A request times out.

8
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:18,587
Did the card get charged?

9
00:00:18,587 --> 00:00:21,062
Retry, and you may charge twice.

10
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:22,725
Fail over to a backup provider,

11
00:00:22,725 --> 00:00:24,562
and you may charge twice.

12
00:00:24,562 --> 00:00:26,400
Webhooks arrive more than once,

13
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,600
and a replayed job can post
the same success twice,

14
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,300
so the ledger drifts.

15
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,975
Most hard payment bugs live in that gap.

16
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:37,025
Railvanta runs the whole lifecycle:

17
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:40,250
an idempotent API, a leased outbox,

18
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:42,000
a deterministic mock provider,

19
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,850
a double entry ledger, and reconciliation.

20
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:49,250
It is a Go API on SQLite, a
Next.js operator dashboard,

21
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:51,813
and a Playwright black box test suite.

22
00:00:51,813 --> 00:00:53,650
Everything is a sandbox.

23
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:55,350
No real money moves.

24
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:58,375
One click starts a sandbox session,

25
00:00:58,375 --> 00:01:02,287
and the dashboard opens already
holding simulated sample runs,

26
00:01:02,287 --> 00:01:04,550
one for each of six failures.

27
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:07,750
The central case: the
provider charges the card,

28
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:09,675
but the response is lost.

29
00:01:09,675 --> 00:01:11,675
Railvanta does not mark it failed,

30
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:13,250
and does not retry.

31
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:14,900
The payment becomes unknown,

32
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:16,538
which is a real state.

33
00:01:16,538 --> 00:01:19,163
A recovery worker asks the same provider,

34
00:01:19,163 --> 00:01:21,325
using the stored idempotency key,

35
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:23,662
and the payment resolves to succeeded.

36
00:01:23,662 --> 00:01:27,325
The invariants tab shows
exactly one succeeded charge,

37
00:01:27,325 --> 00:01:29,700
and no attempt on a second provider.

38
00:01:30,225 --> 00:01:34,837
Ten concurrent requests with the same
idempotency key become one payment,

39
00:01:34,837 --> 00:01:37,313
one attempt, and one charge.

40
00:01:37,313 --> 00:01:41,550
The same key with a changed amount
is rejected with a four oh nine.

41
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:45,475
When the provider is down,
failing over is tempting.

42
00:01:45,475 --> 00:01:48,187
But the first provider
may already have charged.

43
00:01:48,187 --> 00:01:50,125
So the payment stays unknown,

44
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:51,275
and visible.

45
00:01:51,275 --> 00:01:54,875
Railvanta never fails
over an ambiguous outcome.

46
00:01:55,425 --> 00:01:58,500
Reconciliation compares
settlement records.

47
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:02,700
When they disagree, it records
the discrepancy with its evidence.

48
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,725
It never edits the ledger
to make the numbers match.

49
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,375
Every guarantee is checked
against stored evidence,

50
00:02:09,375 --> 00:02:12,125
by the backend and by the automated tests,

51
00:02:12,125 --> 00:02:15,750
including tests that
kill the API mid payment.

52
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:20,450
Real providers, scale, and production
auth are deliberately out of scope.

53
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:23,550
The point is the correctness
of the orchestration.
